# Untitled

Selective Turn-On Ammonia Sensing Enabled by High-Temperature Fluorescence in Metal−Organic Frameworks with Open Metal Sites
†Department of Chemistry and ‡Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts 02139, United States
## \*S Supporting Information
One of the premier performance metrics in chemical sensing applications is chemical selectivity, the ability to detect a given molecular species while avoiding false positives from other potential intererents. When high selectivity is coupled with low detection limits and with signal transduction mechanisms that allow for facile device implementation, chemical sensing enables a range of applications in the defense, food packaging, and environmental monitoring sectors, among others. Microporous MOFs are attractive for this purpose because, in principle, they can be tuned to detect only specific analytes, thus offering good selectivity, and they can exhibit a high density of analyte adsorption/binding sites, thereby enabling lower detection limits. Although various signal transduction mechanisms have been probed in MOFs, those based on changes in luminescence are favored thus far, partly because fluorescence detection obviates the necessity for film fabrication or other processing, a significant challenge with these materials. Despite the potential advantages, fluorescent MOF sensors typically operate by luminescence quenching, a "turn-off" mechanism that is seldom specific and is
generally more difficult to detect. A much more attractive mechanism involves fluorescence "turn-on", where interaction of the MOF with an analyte either turns a "dark" material into a luminescent one or causes a shift from the initial maximum wavelength of the emission peak, \( \lambda_{\mathrm{i}} \), to a final value, \( \lambda_{\mathrm{f}} \). Monitoring the luminescence response at this single wavelength \( \lambda_{\mathrm{f}} \) is read as an increase in the luminescence intensity (Figure 1). MOFs that display luminescence turn-on upon contact with
analytes are rare; those that exhibit both turn-on fluorescence and selectivity for a single chemical species are rarer still.
Here, we demonstrate that fluorescent MOFs maintain their luminescence up to high temperatures, in contrast to molecular crystals or polymers, which often lose their fluorescence near their melting or glass transition temperature. Notably, the high-temperature fluorescence exhibited by the two materials studied here enables the selective detection of gaseous ammonia at \( 100\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C} \). Interaction of \( \mathrm{NH}_{3} \) with \( \mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TCPE}) \) (1) and \( \mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DHBC}) \) (2) causes fluorescence shifts and turn-on luminescence responses preferentially over a variety of potential interferents such as water, methanol, larger amines, and other gases. The \( \mathrm{NH}_{3} \) sensing response can be reversed in 2 upon short evacuation without additional heating, suggesting potential applications in reversible \( \mathrm{NH}_{3} \) sensors.
Fluorescent organic crystals and polymers typically become non-emitting upon melting or glassifying, such that their use in luminescence-based devices can be limited even at moderately high temperatures. We showed recently that one means to
modulate the luminescence response of small molecules, such as tetraphenylethylene (TPE), is to incorporate them within rigid MOFs such as 1 (Figure S1),\(^{20,21}\) where vibrational modes responsible for fluorescence quenching are minimized.\(^{22}\) Here, we show that these vibrational modes are suppressed even at higher temperatures and that 1 maintains its fluorescence up to at least \(350\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) close to its decomposition temperature of 400 \(^{\circ}{\cal C},\) as determined by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Figure S2). To investigate the temperature-dependent luminescence of 1, we collected emission spectra of a powdered sample as it was gradually heated in air inside of a reaction chamber (see Supporting Information for additional details).\(^{23}\) The fluorescence intensity, \(I,\) at each temperature point was divided by the intensity observed at room temperature, \(I_{0},\) and is plotted as \(I/I_{0}\) vs temperature in Figure 2, which also displays data for a
cooling cycle. As shown in the figure, 1 loses \(50\%\) of its initial fluorescence intensity only at \(T_{1/2}\,=\,190\,\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\) Furthermore, 1 does not lose its crystallinity or luminescence response upon cooling and repeating the cycle at least three times. This is in stark contrast with TPE itself, also shown in Figure 2, which under identical experimental conditions dramatically loses its fluorescence \(\left(T_{1/2}\;=\;83\;\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)\) . In fact, TPE becomes virtually non-fluorescent near its melting point of \(224\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) when presumably its low-energy vibrational modes responsible for fluorescence quenching are reactivated.\(^{24}\) Thus, TPE-borne luminescence persists to a much higher temperature when TPE is inserted in a rigid MOF. At \(200\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) for instance, \(I/I_{0}\) for 1 was \(47\%\) , approximately 5 times larger than that of TPE, which was only \(10\%\) .
We surmised that the temperature-persistent luminescence of 1 could be used to modulate its response toward various gasphase analytes. Thus, exposure of activated 1 to ammonia, triethylamine, ethylenediamine (en), \(N,N.\) -diethylformamide (DEF), and water vapors at room temperature shifted its emission maximum by \(0{-}23\ \mathrm{nm}\) . However, as shown in Figure 3, monitoring the effect of these analytes at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) showed that only ammonia exposure led to a significant shift of the emission maximum from 487 to \(511\,\textrm{n m},\) thus revealing a remarkable selectivity for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) detection. Unfortunately, ammonia binding also caused an irreversible phase change in 1 to an unidentified crystalline form (Figure S3), which suggested that this material would not be effective in a reversible sensing application.
However, we were interested in the mechanism leading to the preferential sensing. We reasoned that the selectivity may be due to the differential strength of analyte binding to the \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) ions in the \(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}-)\) , secondary building units of 1. To test this hypothesis, we calculated the binding energies of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to the paddlewheel unit using density functional theory (DFT) and found that \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) binding was more favorable than \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) binding by \(38\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) (Table S1 and Figure S4). This finding suggested that, although both \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) may affect the electronic structure of the \(\mathrm{TCPE}^{4-}\) by binding to \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) in 1, only the former does so effectively at \(100\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C}.\) More importantly, these results also implied that the selectivity may be observed in other thermally stable, fluorescent MOFs with open metal sites and that a reversible \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) sensor may be found among reported materials that fit these criteria.
Although damage to the crystal during this treatment prevented the collection of a full data set, we used the new unit cell parameters to model the structure of 2d using an original Matlab routine that we reported recently (Figure S8).26 As shown in Figures 4 and S7, the changes evidenced in the PXRD patterns are caused by a relatively small breathing mode that occurs upon desolvation but does not disrupt the original bonding of as-synthesized 2.
Having ascertained that the connectivity of 2 remains intact upon desolvation, we tested the response of 2d to various gas-phase analytes at temperatures up to \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). We were intrigued to discover that 2d had been reported as a selective \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorbent.25 Accordingly, we investigated its fluorescence response upon exposure to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) but found no changes in its emission profile at \(-78\), 25, or \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figures S9−S11). In contrast, exposure of 2d to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) gas at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for less than 1 min caused a significant shift in its emission maximum (Figures 5 and S12−S14). No changes were observed upon exposure to methanol or water vapors at this temperature even after \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) (Figures 5 and S15, S16). The lack of changes in the emission profile of 2d upon water exposure at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was in line with the TGA under controlled humidity and with PXRD studies (Figure S17). Significantly, short-term exposure of 2d to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) at this temperature did not cause any structural changes, as verified by PXRD (Figure S18), and the emission maximum could be reversed to the original wavelength by evacuating the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-exposed sample for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) (Figure S14). To further test
the potential damage caused by \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) exposure, we immersed samples of 2d in DMSO- \(d_{6}\) before and after the ammonia exposure. As shown in Figure S19, neither experiment showed evidence of ligand in solution, as might be expected upon leakage of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DHBDC}^{2-}\) from damaged samples. Together, these results show that ammonia sensing in 2d is selective over important atmospheric interferents, including \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The selective detection of ammonia is reversible upon evacuation, portending potential applications in real devices.
To investigate the mechanism that gives rise to selectivity in 2, we performed in situ IR studies under conditions mimicking those employed for the fluorescence measurements. Thus, IR spectroscopy of 2d under \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) exposure at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) revealed new peaks that could be associated with unbound \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) molecules (Figure S20) but did not reveal the presence of \(\mathrm{Mg-N}\) stretches \(\left(\nu_{\mathrm{Mg-N}}\right)\). As such, we considered alternative sensing mechanisms and addressed the possibility of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) interacting directly with the ligand, especially with the free \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups. To examine whether the free ligand, \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DHBDC}\), could itself function as a reversible ammonia sensor, for instance, a sample was exposed to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) vapors at room temperature and at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In contrast to 2d, where an emission shift and a turn-on response were recorded, exposure of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DHBDC}\) to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) caused the formation of a new, nonemissive, crystalline material (Figure 5). We were able to identify this new material as the ammonium salt \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\right)\)-\(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{DHBDC}\right)\) by independently synthesizing it from \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DHBDC}\) and aqueous ammonium hydroxide and confirming its structure by X-ray crystallography (Table S3, Figure S21).
Because treatment of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DHBDC}\) with \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) did not cause deprotonation of the phenol groups, we do not expect that a chemical interaction between \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) and the ligand itself is responsible for the turn-on response observed in 2d. Furthermore, the carboxylate groups in 2d are engaged in bonding to \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) and are obviously not available for further deprotonation. Therefore, we propose that the selective response is indeed due to direct binding of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) to the \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) sites, and that the changes in the electronic structure at the metal site inductively affect the HOMO and LUMO energies of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DHBDC}^{2-}\) linker. Although a charge-transfer mechanism whereby \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) molecules affect the orbital energies of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DHBDC}^{2-}\) directly (i.e., through space) cannot be ruled out, such a mechanism should also be activated upon exposure to water vapor. Furthermore, the absence of \(\nu_{\mathrm{Mg-N}}\) bands need not indicate the lack of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) binding at \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) sites. Instead, it may be due to the low concentration of \(\mathrm{Mg-NH}_{3}\) pairs or the concealment of \(\nu_{\mathrm{Mg-N}}\) by stronger framework bands in the low-frequency/far-IR region. As with 1, this hypothesis is also
supported by DFT calculations of the interaction of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) with a truncated model of 2d, which showed that ammonia binding is more favorable than water binding by \(19\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) (Table S1). Although our calculations suggest that methanol and ammonia should have similar binding energies, we partially ascribe the selectivity for the former to the small pores of \(2\mathbf{d}^{25}\) and the larger kinetic diameter, \(k_{\mathrm{D}}\) of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) \((k_{\mathrm{D}}\;=\;3.8{-}4.1\;\;\mathring{\mathrm{A}})^{27}\) compared to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) \((k_{\mathrm{D}}=2.6\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})^{28}\) and water \((k_{\mathrm{D}}=2.65\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) .29 A combination of molecular sieving and chemical selectivity is therefore responsible for the unique \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) sensing properties of 2d.
The foregoing results demonstrate that MOFs remain emissive up to high temperatures, a property that is likely general for many fluorescent MOFs because of their rigidity. This stands on its own as an interesting effect that is seldom replicated in organic molecular crystals or flexible polymers and may serve to study the photophysics of chromophores under unusual conditions. Here, we use this effect to show that temperature can be employed as an easily tunable parameter to increase the chemical selectivity in molecular sensing applications. In doing so, we demonstrate a new, reversible, and selective sensor for ammonia, an important environmental pollutant.
